  "methods": {
    "source_requested_quantity_first": "Inventoried every modern exercise's requested quantity before looking up source table cells; no standard answer or choice key used to infer intent",
    "XIV_layout": "m row labels preserve their printed leading comma; main columns 0-9 are separate from italic correction columns 1-9",
    "XIV_lookup": "For a fractional exponent with four places, first two decimal digits determine row, third determines main column, fourth (if nonzero) selects the same-row correction. Add that literal integer to the four printed main digits, then place the decimal after the first digit.",
    "no_correction_case": "For a three-place exponent or a trailing fourth zero, the exponent lies on a main grid point; use that main entry directly. Null correction below means no correction lookup, not an unread cell.",
    "negative_decomposition_and_scaling": "For x=k+m with integer k and 0<=m<1, 10^x=10^k*10^m. The lesson itself gives -2.685=-3+0.315 and first-digit decimal placement. Applying ordinary exponent and decimal arithmetic needs no separately invented external negative-decomposition gate.",
    "IV_scale": "Use an even power-of-ten radicand scaling. In particular sqrt(0.1)=sqrt(10)/10, so the read 3.162 gives 0.3162 without computing a new root.",
    "precision": "Four printed significant digits in main entries. Main-entry rounding and row-wise fourth-digit correction are distinct; corrected values are not asserted to be correctly rounded direct powers.",
    "integer_exponents": "10^-1=0.1, 10^0=1, 10^1=10 exactly by integer-power arithmetic, not additional table cells",
    "rounding_680": "After exact exponent reduction and table lookup, round the requested terms to two decimal places. No result is a rounding tie.",
